  "quote_of_the_week": {
    "type": "Quote of the Week",
    "quote": "Life is what happens to you while you are busy making other plans.",
    "author": "John Lennon (9 October 1940 - 8 December 1980) - English musician & activist."
  },
  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "The Lunar New Year (LNY) effect refers to a persistent calendar anomaly where stock markets in \nChina and surrounding regions typically experience higher positive returns around the holiday, driven \nby increased investor optimism. This trend appears to extend to the shipping market as well, with \nparticularly strong demand from Chinese buyers for tonnage. Consequently, we can expect a busy \nFebruary ahead.",
    "bulkers": "Japanese cape-size duo ALLY & MIKATA (both built in 2005) were sold together for $37 \nmillion. The one-year younger, scrubber fitted caper ROBUSTO (174k built 2006 in SWS/China) \nachieved a higher price of $19.5 million, with delivery scheduled for Q2 this year in the Far East.  \nThe scrubber-fitted post-panamax JP CARETTA (88k built 2008 in Japan) set a new market \nbenchmark, selling for approximately $13.5 to $13.8 million. \nThe Kamsarmax vessel RIZE (82k built 2012 in S. Korea) sold for $17.7 million, while the Panamax \nBULK XAYMACA (76k built 2006 in Japan) was committed at around $9.2 to $9.5 million.  \nGreek buyers acquired the ultramax MITSOS (63k built 2013 in China) for $20.5 million.  \nThree supramaxes built in 2008 changed hands: Almi's ANASA (56k built in Mitsui, Japan, m/e B& \nW) at just over $13 million, Tess 58 FLORINDA (58k built in Zhoushan, m/e B& W) at $12.6 million, \nand Costamare's CLARA (56k built in IHI, Japan, m/e Wartsila) at $11.8 million, possibly reflecting \nengine preferences.  \nChinese buyers paid the firm price of $9 million for the Norwegian-controlled SPAR CANIS (53k \nbuilt 2006 in Chengxi, China) and only $8.1 million for the younger, Indonesian-controlled MDM \nBROMO (53k built 2007 in Dayang, China).  \nGreek buyers paid $18.4 million for the handy DARYA TAPTI (36k built 2015 in Shikoku, Japan), \nfitted with energy-saving features. The Korean-built handy JETSTREAM (34k built 2012 in SPP) \nsold for $13.5 million, while two Chinese-built handy sisters, YANGTZE GRACE and YANGTZE \nHAPPINESS (32k built 2012 in JNS), were sold domestically for $10 million each.",
    "demolition": "Subcontinent rates are slightly improved this week with positive sentiment. For an \noverview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
